 /*
- * MAX77705 USB-C/BC1.2 input current selection
+ * MAX77705 USB-C/BC1.2 input current selection and 9 V USB-PD
  *
  * The MAX77705's USB-C interface block (I2C 0x25) runs its own CC/PD
- * firmware and reports what is attached: the BC1.2 charger type and the
- * Type-C current the source advertises on CC. The charger block (driven by
- * max77705_charger) never learns about either, so its input current limit
- * stays at the chip's power-on default (500 or 900 mA) and a 3 A wall
- * charger ends up barely charging a tablet in use.
+ * firmware: it detects the BC1.2 charger type and the Type-C current a
+ * source advertises, and on its own agrees a 5 V USB-PD contract. The
+ * charger block (driven by max77705_charger) never learns about any of
+ * it, so its input current stays at the chip's power-on default (500 or
+ * 900 mA) and a 3 A wall charger ends up barely charging a tablet in use.
  *
- * This driver only reads that status, on the block's interrupt, and sets
- * the charger's input and charge current to the matching values of the
- * Galaxy Tab S7's stock battery table, once the status has been stable for
- * 300 ms (a PC port's advertisement can flip for ~0.5 s after attach). It
- * never commands the USB-C firmware: no PD contract is requested, VBUS
- * stays at 5 V.
+ * This driver reads that status on the block's interrupt and sets the
+ * charger's input and charge current from the Galaxy Tab S7's stock
+ * battery table, once it has been stable for 300 ms (a PC port's
+ * advertisement can flip for ~0.5 s after attach).
+ *
+ * With a PD source it also moves the contract to the source's fixed 9 V
+ * PDO, as stock does through the same firmware commands: it reads the
+ * source's PDOs (OPCODE_CURRENT_SRCCAP), sets the 9 V input limit, and
+ * requests the PDO (OPCODE_SRCCAP_REQUEST). The 9 V charge current is only
+ * set once the firmware reports the new contract ready and its own VBUS
+ * ADC reads 8.5-9.5 V; otherwise the 5 V limits come back. Only fixed
+ * PDOs up to 9 V are used (stock AVAILABLE_VOLTAGE); PPS is not.
+ *
+ * Charging follows the stock battery temperature bands: no 9 V and a lower
+ * charge current outside 15-42 C, a trickle outside 0-50 C. The battery
+ * temperature comes from the battery thermistor on the PMIC ADC (stock's
+ * "adc-temp", PM8150L AMUX_THM1_100K_PU); the fuel gauge's own reading is
+ * not wired to it on this board. Without a plausible reading: no 9 V and
+ * at most 1000 mA.
  */
 
